<h2>Packaging ML Models into Lambda Layers</h2>

<p>Lambda has a 250MB deployment package limit, though larger models are stored in S3 and downloaded at runtime. Layers decouple dependencies from function code, enabling reuse across functions.</p>

<div class="infographic">
  <strong>Lambda Layer Architecture</strong><br><br>
  Function Code (50KB) + Model Layer (100MB) + Dependencies Layer (50MB) + Runtime Code<br>
  Max unzipped size: 262MB | Layers enable independent versioning
</div>

<p><strong>Model Storage Strategy</strong>: For models exceeding 250MB, download from S3 at function startup. Implement instance-level caching so warm invocations skip the download.</p>

<div class="code"><span class="cm"># Layer structure for Python dependencies</span><br><span class="st">python-deps-layer/</span><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="st">python/lib/python3.11/site-packages/</span><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="nm">numpy</span><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="nm">transformers</span><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="nm">torch</span></div>

<p>Layers must follow the directory structure <code>python/lib/pythonX.Y/site-packages/</code> or <code>nodejs/node_modules/</code>. AWS Lambda automatically adds layer contents to the code path.</p>

<div class="callout">
<strong>Instance Reuse:</strong> Lambda containers persist for ~5-15 minutes. Module-level variable initialization (outside handler) only runs on cold start. Use this for model loading to avoid reloading on every invocation.
</div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="cold-starts">
<h2>Cold Start Optimization</h2>

<p>Cold starts occur when Lambda must create a new container, download code, and initialize the runtime. For ML models, this can take 5-30 seconds. Production systems must minimize this latency.</p>

<div class="infographic">
  <strong>Cold Start Timeline</strong><br><br>
  1. Container spin-up (1-2s) → 2. Runtime init (0.5-1s) → 3. Code download (1-3s) → 4. Model load (2-10s)<br>
  Total: 4-16 seconds typical | Warm start: 10-100ms
</div>

<p><strong>Optimization Strategies:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Use Smaller Models</strong>: Replace BERT with DistilBERT when accuracy permits</li>
<li><strong>Quantization</strong>: 4-bit or 8-bit quantized models load faster</li>
<li><strong>Container Image Caching</strong>: Lambda keeps containers warm for recent requests</li>
<li><strong>Provisioned Concurrency</strong>: Pre-warm containers by maintaining minimum active invocations</li>
<li><strong>Lazy Loading</strong>: Load model only on first request, cache in memory</li>
</ol>

<div class="callout warning">
<strong>Memory Trade-off:</strong> Increasing memory (up to 10GB) increases CPU allocation proportionally, speeding up model loading. Higher memory = faster execution but higher cost. Test empirically.
</div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="api-gateway">
<h2>API Gateway Integration</h2>

<p>API Gateway provides REST and HTTP APIs fronting Lambda, handling authentication, throttling, and request transformation. This creates a production-ready inference endpoint accessible over HTTP.</p>

<p><strong>Request Transformation</strong>: Map HTTP requests to Lambda event format, handle response serialization automatically. Define request/response models for validation and documentation.</p>

<div class="callout success">
<strong>HTTP API vs REST:</strong> Use HTTP API for simple workloads (50% cheaper, lower latency). Use REST for complex authorization, request transformation, and caching needs.
</div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="best-practices">
<h2>Best Practices for ML on Lambda</h2>

<p><strong>Batch Processing</strong>: For non-real-time scenarios, use SQS to queue requests and process batches, amortizing cold start costs.</p>

<p><strong>Monitoring</strong>: CloudWatch logs capture duration, memory usage, and errors. Create custom metrics for model inference latency.</p>

<p><strong>Cost Management</strong>: Monitor GB-seconds usage. For sustained high traffic, ECS or Kubernetes become more economical.</p>

<div class="callout">
<strong>Concurrency Management:</strong> Reserved concurrency guarantees capacity. Throttle with reserved concurrency to protect downstream systems. Enable concurrent function invocations up to account limits.
</div>
</section>

